==English==

===Etymology 1===
From {{term|Eid}}.

====Noun====
{{en-noun|-}}

# {{alternative capitalization of|Eid}}

===Etymology 2===
From English dialectal {{term|eid|lang=en}}, from {{etyl|non}} {{term|eið||an isthmus, neck of land|lang=non}}, from {{proto|Germanic|aidan|isthmus, strait|lang=en}}, of unknown origin. Perhaps from {{proto|Indo-European|h₁y-|go|lang=en}}. Cognate with {{etyl|no|-}} {{term|eid||isthmus|lang=no}}, {{etyl|sv|-}} {{term|ed|lang=sv}}, {{etyl|fo|-}} {{term|eið||isthmus|lang=fo}}.

====Alternative forms====
* {{l|en|ed}}, {{l|en|aith}}

====Noun====
{{en-noun}}

# {{UK|_|dialectal|Scotland}} An [[isthmus]] or narrow neck of land jutting out into the sea; a [[sandbank]] cast up by the sea across the head of an open [[bight]] or inlet and having a lagoon inside it.

